WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.904
Prompt injection can lead to several kinds of harm.

2
00:00:02.904 --> 00:00:15.775
A model might reveal information it should not disclose, ignore application instructions, summarize content incorrectly, produce misleading outputs, or take unsafe actions through connected tools.

3
00:00:15.775 --> 00:00:28.666
The risk increases when an LLM application has access to email, files, tickets, cloud systems, databases, code repositories, customer records, or business workflows.

4
00:00:28.666 --> 00:00:34.303
In those designs, model behavior can influence real actions or sensitive resources.

5
00:00:34.303 --> 00:00:37.696
Teams should distinguish model confusion from system impact.

6
00:00:37.696 --> 00:00:41.627
A strange answer may be low impact in a simple chatbot.

7
00:00:41.627 --> 00:00:50.976
The same behavior can be high impact if the application passes model output to tools, workflow automation, approvals, or downstream systems.

8
00:00:50.976 --> 00:00:54.618
The most important question is not only whether the model can be tricked.

9
00:00:54.618 --> 00:00:58.260
It is what the application allows the model to do after it is tricked.

10
00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:03.818
Strong system design limits the damage when the model processes hostile or confusing input.
